% ============================================================================
% simple-machine-alt-example — for the simple machines where NASA lists a
% SECOND everyday example, that second example (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `simple-machines.adj`
% (`simple_machine_example(machine, example)`, ONE everyday example per
% simple machine): a new `simple_machine_alt_example(machine, alt_example)`
% table names the SECOND everyday example the SAME already-cited NASA
% educator-notes page lists for a machine, decoded from spans already sitting
% unused inside `simple-machines.adj`'s own header and provenance block — no
% new WebFetch. That table's `example` column has room for only the FIRST
% everyday example; the SAME already-quoted NASA sentences also name a
% second everyday object for five of the six machines that never made it
% into a row:
%
%   lever → scissors
%     "Lever: ... (examples: seesaw, scissors)"
%   inclined_plane → stairs
%     "Inclined Plane: ... (examples: ramp, stairs)"
%   wedge → knife
%     "Wedge: ... (examples: door stop, knife)"
%   wheel_and_axle → clock
%     "Wheel and axle: ... (examples: bicycle, clock)"
%   pulley → water_well
%     "Pulley: ... (examples: elevator, water well)"
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? simple_machine_alt_example(lever, $AltEx)      % scissors
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `simple-machines.adj`'s own `simple_machine_example`): each `row` lowers to
% a ground relation `simple_machine_alt_example(machine, alt_example)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the alternate example WITH its source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on `screw` (the machine's own
% `simple-machines.adj` row already uses NASA's SECOND listed example,
% "bottle caps", as the primary example — because the FIRST listed example
% for screw is literally the word "screw" itself, not a distinct everyday
% object — so screw has no unused second example left to decode here).
%
% Only FIVE of `simple-machines.adj`'s six machines have a genuinely
% second, distinct everyday example in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table
% is deliberately narrow rather than inventing an example the source does
% not state. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an alternate example and
% recall which machine it is an instance of:
%
%     ? simple_machine_alt_example($M, stairs)   % inclined_plane
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME lever span already quoted
% inside `simple-machines.adj`'s own provenance block, from the SAME NASA
% Next Gen STEM "Simple Machines" classroom-connection educator notes that
% table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a
% part of each already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance
% envelope (per `simple-machines.adj`'s own convention), so
% `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the lever span; the other four rows'
% own spans are listed above for independent auditability. `trust
% authoritative` — the same tier `simple-machines.adj` already carries (a
% primary U.S. government NASA education page).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table simple_machine_alt_example {
    columns machine, alt_example

    row (lever, scissors)
    row (inclined_plane, stairs)
    row (wedge, knife)
    row (wheel_and_axle, clock)
    row (pulley, water_well)

    source "Lever: uses a surface situated on a fulcrum (pivoting point) to move an object (examples: seesaw, scissors)"
    locator "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/simple_machines_classroom_connection_508.pdf"
    trust authoritative
}
